From 1d92fec2fea5f2e2fe94fcf96133d056f39576c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AmirSalahY <amir@noury.ch>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 12:05:51 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 20/20] fix(ui): don't mark the user's own shell aliases as
 invalid commands
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The command-line highlighter validated a command-position word against
$PATH and a builtin list, so `gst` or `ll` rendered as invalidCommand
even though the shell runs them fine.

Alias names were already known to the runtime, but only through
loadedAliases, which is populated solely when useAliases is on. That
config gates *expansion* — rewriting what gets completed — which is a
different question from whether the word names a real command. So split
reading the shell's aliases from expanding them: names are always read
for bash/zsh, expansion stays behind useAliases, and the highlighter
reads a separate set covering shell aliases plus managed ones.

The alias check sits ahead of the commandExists memo cache. Shell
aliases load asynchronously and managed aliases can appear mid-session,
so a miss cached during the first keystrokes would otherwise pin a real
command red for the rest of the session.

getAliasNames() is left alone, so suggestions don't start offering shell
aliases when expansion is switched off.
